Task 1: Prepare the Client System for IdM Enrollment
Subtask 1.1: Verify Network Connectivity and DNS Resolution
First, we need to ensure the client system can communicate with the IdM server and resolve DNS names properly.

Check network connectivity to IdM server:
ping -c 4 ipa-server.example.com
Verify DNS resolution:
nslookup ipa-server.example.com
dig ipa-server.example.com
Check reverse DNS lookup:
nslookup 192.168.1.10
Replace 192.168.1.10 with your IdM server's IP address

Verify the client's hostname configuration:
hostname -f
hostnamectl status
Subtask 1.2: Configure System Prerequisites
Update the system packages:
sudo dnf update -y
Install required packages for IdM client:
sudo dnf install -y ipa-client sssd-tools krb5-workstation
Configure the system timezone (should match IdM server):
sudo timedatectl set-timezone America/New_York
timedatectl status
Ensure NTP synchronization:
sudo systemctl enable chronyd
sudo systemctl start chronyd
chrony sources -v
Subtask 1.3: Configure DNS Settings
Edit the DNS configuration:
sudo vi /etc/resolv.conf
Add the IdM server as the primary DNS server:
nameserver 192.168.1.10
search example.com
Replace 192.168.1.10 with your IdM server's IP address

Make DNS configuration persistent (for NetworkManager):
sudo nmcli connection modify "System eth0" ipv4.dns "192.168.1.10"
sudo nmcli connection modify "System eth0" ipv4.dns-search "example.com"
sudo nmcli connection up "System eth0"
Task 2: Enroll RHEL Client System Using ipa-client-install
Subtask 2.1: Discover IdM Server Information
Test IdM server discovery:
ipa-client-install --discovery-autodiscovery
Manual discovery if autodiscovery fails:
ipa-client-install --server=ipa-server.example.com --domain=example.com --realm=EXAMPLE.COM --discovery
Subtask 2.2: Perform Client Enrollment
Run the interactive client installation:
sudo ipa-client-install --hostname=client.example.com --mkhomedir
Follow the interactive prompts:

Continue to configure the system with these values? Type yes
User authorized to enroll computers: Enter admin
Password for admin@EXAMPLE.COM: Enter the admin password
Alternative non-interactive installation:

sudo ipa-client-install \
  --hostname=client.example.com \
  --server=ipa-server.example.com \
  --domain=example.com \
  --realm=EXAMPLE.COM \
  --principal=admin \
  --password=YourAdminPassword \
  --mkhomedir \
  --unattended
Subtask 2.3: Verify Client Enrollment
Check the enrollment status:
sudo ipa-client-install --uninstall --dry-run
Verify Kerberos configuration:
cat /etc/krb5.conf
Check SSSD configuration:
sudo cat /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
Verify client certificate:
sudo ls -la /etc/ipa/
sudo ipa-getcert list
Task 3: Configure SSSD for Kerberos Authentication
Subtask 3.1: Examine SSSD Configuration
Review the default SSSD configuration:
sudo cat /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
Understanding key SSSD parameters:
id_provider: Specifies identity information source
auth_provider: Defines authentication method
chpass_provider: Password change provider
cache_credentials: Enables offline authentication
Subtask 3.2: Customize SSSD Configuration
Create a backup of the original configuration:
sudo cp /etc/sssd/sssd.conf /etc/sssd/sssd.conf.backup
Edit SSSD configuration for enhanced security:
sudo vi /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
Add or modify the following sections:
[sssd]
config_file_version = 2
services = nss, pam, ssh, sudo
domains = example.com

[domain/example.com]
id_provider = ipa
auth_provider = ipa
access_provider = ipa
chpass_provider = ipa
ipa_server = ipa-server.example.com
ipa_domain = example.com
ipa_hostname = client.example.com
cache_credentials = True
krb5_store_password_if_offline = True
ldap_tls_cacert = /etc/ipa/ca.crt

[nss]
homedir_substring = /home

[pam]
offline_credentials_expiration = 0

[sudo]

[ssh]
Subtask 3.3: Configure PAM for Home Directory Creation
Enable automatic home directory creation:
sudo authselect select sssd with-mkhomedir --force
Verify PAM configuration:
sudo authselect current
Alternative manual PAM configuration:
sudo vi /etc/pam.d/system-auth
Add the following line after the pam_unix.so line:

session optional pam_mkhomedir.so skel=/etc/skel umask=077
Subtask 3.4: Restart and Enable SSSD Services
Restart SSSD service:
sudo systemctl restart sssd
Enable SSSD to start at boot:
sudo systemctl enable sssd
Check SSSD service status:
sudo systemctl status sssd
Verify SSSD is running properly:
sudo sssctl status
Task 4: Verify Kerberos Authentication Using kinit
Subtask 4.1: Test Kerberos Authentication
Initialize Kerberos credentials for admin user:
kinit admin
Enter the admin password when prompted

List current Kerberos tickets:
klist
Verify ticket details:
klist -v
Subtask 4.2: Test User Authentication
Create a test user on the IdM server (if not already created):
ipa user-add testuser --first=Test --last=User --password
Test authentication with the new user:
kinit testuser@EXAMPLE.COM
Verify the user can obtain tickets:
klist
Test SSH login with Kerberos:
ssh testuser@client.example.com
Subtask 4.3: Test SSSD User Resolution
Test user information retrieval:
id testuser
getent passwd testuser
Test group information:
getent group
groups testuser
Test sudo access (if configured):
sudo -u testuser whoami
Subtask 4.4: Verify Offline Authentication
Cache user credentials:
su - testuser
exit
Simulate network disconnection (optional):
sudo systemctl stop NetworkManager
Test offline authentication:
su - testuser
Restore network connection:
sudo systemctl start NetworkManager
Troubleshooting Common Issues
DNS Resolution Problems
Issue: Client cannot resolve IdM server hostname Solution:

# Check DNS configuration
cat /etc/resolv.conf
# Test DNS resolution
nslookup ipa-server.example.com
# Restart NetworkManager
sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager
Time Synchronization Issues
Issue: Kerberos authentication fails due to time skew Solution:

# Check time synchronization
timedatectl status
# Synchronize time manually
sudo chronyd -q 'server ipa-server.example.com iburst'
# Restart chronyd
sudo systemctl restart chronyd
SSSD Service Issues
Issue: SSSD fails to start or authenticate users Solution:

# Check SSSD logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/sssd/sssd.log
# Clear SSSD cache
sudo sss_cache -E
# Restart SSSD
sudo systemctl restart sssd
Certificate Problems
Issue: SSL/TLS certificate verification fails Solution:

# Check certificate status
sudo ipa-getcert list
# Refresh certificates
sudo ipa-getcert resubmit -i <request-id>
# Verify CA certificate
openssl x509 -in /etc/ipa/ca.crt -text -noout
